WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.230
If you want to hear how the mythological monster
The Wendigo from the Pet Sematary novel was

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.900
hidden throughout this movie, then stick around
to the end of this video.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.730
The opening of Mary Lambert’s 1989 adaptation
of Pet Sematary contains a montage of kids

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:20.500
over the years laying their pets down to rest
in the Pet Sematary, and this scene contains

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.369
a lot of easter eggs referencing some of the
pets memorialized in the Sematary in Stephen

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:25.369
King’s novel.

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:28.700
For example, we hear a young Jud saying goodbye
to his dog spot…

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.100
"Spot.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.300
A good fellow.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.100
We love you."

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.850
And see the grave, which was carved by a Mr.
Judson Crandle.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:40.530
There are also grave markers for Hannah, who
is regarded as as “the best dog to that

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:41.530
ever lived.”

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:45.030
The book mentions that Hannah died in 1939,
making this one of the oldest readable grave

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.030
markers.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.210
One of the more recent ones was Biffer, which
you can see right here.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.890
Smucky the Cat is another Pet that was mentioned
in the book, but Smucky was actually the real

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:55.470
world cat that belonged to Stephen King’s
daughter Naomi, and the cat was struck down

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:56.770
by a truck in Orrington, Maine.

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.930
A short time after, King’s son Owen had
a close call with another truck on that road,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.410
and the incident served as the inspiration
for the novel.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.920
We’re introduced to the road in the movie
with a shot of a truck barreling over the

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:11.080
camera, the low angle foreshadows the view
Gage might have had during his final moments.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.810
Another foreshadowing can be seen in the bumper
stick on Louis’s car, which reads, “Have

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.240
you hugged your MD today?” which could be
a reference to the ending embrace between

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.000
Louis and Zombie Rachel.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
So the Creed family moves into their new home
and their daughter Ellie finds the tire swing.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.080
As she swings she hears a creepy sound coming
from the forest path that leads to the Pet

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.220
Sematary.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.810
If you haven’t read the book, this is easy
enough to chalk up to a moose or something

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:41.510
else in the woods, but those familiar with
the novel may be reminded of the Wendigo,

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:45.549
the Mic Mac legend said to be responsible
for souring the earth upon which Indian burial

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:46.920
ground is located.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.630
In the book, the Wendigo’s power over the
town of Ludlow is talked about several times,

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:54.180
and Louis comes face to face with the creature
when he takes Gage up to the burial ground.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.460
However, in this movie, the Wendigo is not
mentioned or seen, but it is teased at several

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:00.000
more times throughout the film.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.869
Ellie’s tire swing falls, and as Louis and
Rachel tend to her, their son Gage wanders

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:04.869
towards the road.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:07.900
Their new neighbor, Jud Crandall, scoops him
up and gets him to safety.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.849
The next day the family gets to unpacking,
and you can spot the toy Gage is playing with

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:15.560
is actually a red truck, and he crashes it
into a box.

00:02:17.189 --> 00:02:20.900
You may also spot that Gage has a whole box
of toy trucks, which may explain why he’s

00:02:20.909 --> 00:02:24.209
always wandering towards the road, maybe he
just has a fascination with trucks.

00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:27.379
But that’s not the only thing that causes
the disaster to strike.

00:02:27.379 --> 00:02:31.129
The influence of the Wendigo also plays a
part, but we’ll get back to that in a little

00:02:31.129 --> 00:02:32.129
bit.

00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:34.730
As Rachel comes in with the groceries, we
meet the caretaker Missy Dandridge, who tells

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:38.489
Rachel she was lucky to marry to doctor, and
that she wishes she had a doctor to help with

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:39.599
her stomach pains.

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:41.440
She also mentions she’s not married.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.049
In the book, Missy is married to man named
Roger, or possibly David -- the name erroneously

00:02:46.049 --> 00:02:47.879
changes halfway through the book.

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:50.590
Later that day Jud shows them the way to the
Pet Sematary.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:54.469
Louis carries Gage in the baby backpack, and
this would not be the first time that he carries

00:02:54.469 --> 00:02:55.950
his son up that path.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:59.560
Rachel is uncomfortable bringing the kids
to the sematary because of her aversion to

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.560
death.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.749
In the background you can see the grave marker
for Marta the rabbit.

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:05.931
These graves just goes to show the level of
detail put into the production design, and

00:03:05.931 --> 00:03:08.469
I thought I’d point them out because you
might not have picked them up while watching

00:03:08.469 --> 00:03:09.469
the movie.

00:03:09.469 --> 00:03:12.500
Before they leave, the call of the Wendigo
can be heard again in the woods.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.560
Cut to the real monster of this story...

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:28.199
See Gage, this is why nobody saved you when
you went out in the road.

00:03:28.199 --> 00:03:30.559
Oh man, I’m going to hell.

00:03:30.559 --> 00:03:34.279
So Louis is leaving for his first day of work
at the University and his wife comes to say

00:03:34.279 --> 00:03:35.279
goodbye to him.

00:03:35.279 --> 00:03:38.340
I think there’s something up with the color
yellow foreshadowing death in this movie.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.840
Not only are Rachel and Gage wearing yellow,
but the flowers are yellow -- the house is

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.480
yellow and Church’s carrying case is yellow.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.400
So in this scene, everyone who dies in the
movie has yellow on them.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.719
Missy Dandridge’s sweater isn’t yellow
but she does have yellow flowers on her skirt,

00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:53.799
and the flowers later seen at her memorial
are in fact yellow.

00:03:53.799 --> 00:03:57.159
Another interesting note about this scene
is that Rachel repeatedly called Louis “Doc”,

00:03:57.159 --> 00:04:00.169
which makes sense because he’s a doctor,
but also a partial reference to Danny’s

00:04:00.169 --> 00:04:03.329
nickname in the The Shining.

00:04:03.329 --> 00:04:08.169
“How’d you like some ice cream Doc?”

00:04:08.169 --> 00:04:11.829
Another Stephen King reference can be spotted
in the next scene, where this University student,

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.960
Victor Pascow, is brought into Louis’s care
after being hit by a truck.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:19.280
On the bulletin board, there is a poster warning
about rabies, in which a photo of Cujo, another

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.319
Stephen King adaptation made in the 1980s
can be seen.

00:04:22.319 --> 00:04:26.129
This is more than just a fun reference though,
Jud actually explains in the book that rabies

00:04:26.129 --> 00:04:30.870
is an issue in Maine and even cites the events
of Cujo taking place a few years prior.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.740
So anyways, Louis is unable to save Pascow
because he’s basically DOA. Louis gets a

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.009
big surprise when Pascow unexpectedly jumps
back to life and tells him, “the soil of

00:04:39.009 --> 00:04:40.500
a man’s heart is stonier.”

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.569
This is a recurring line in the book, but
what does it actually mean?

00:04:43.569 --> 00:04:46.840
The full quote in the book is “The soil
of a man’s heart is stonier, Louis…

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:49.020
A man grows what he can and he tends it.”

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:52.580
Stony soil is known to be harder to grow anything
in than fertile soil.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.870
He’s telling Louis that the love he has
for his family cannot be easily replaced -- that

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:00.240
bringing their bodies back to life in the
burial ground does not mean they’d be the

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.440
same people that Louis has grown to love.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:05.849
Pascow’s ghost comes back that night to
try to warn Louis to stay away from the Mic

00:05:05.849 --> 00:05:10.479
Mac burial ground, who are apparently having
a rave of some sort.

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:12.900
Pascow tells Louis the reason that he wants
to help him.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:20.860
“I want to help you, cause, Louis, you tried
to help me.”

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:25.400
OK, I can understand that line in the book,
but in the movie, he just takes one look at

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:26.400
him and gives up.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.860
I mean he puts about as much effort into saving
Pascow as he does that one time he tried to

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.680
rake the leaves.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:42.320
Speaking of the leaves, Thanksgiving gets
here, and the family goes to visit Rachel’s

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:46.030
parents in Chicago, while Louis stays back
to work, when he gets a call from Jud.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:49.240
It’s about Ellie’s cat, Church, and he’s
fallen victim to the road.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:53.460
Jud takes him up to bury the cat, but instead
of stopping at the Pet Sematary, Louis ignore

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:57.010
Pascow’s warnings and follows Jud to the
Mic Mac burial ground.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.920
A strange noise is heard nearby.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:04.340
“Jud!

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.660
What’s that?”

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:07.960
“Shh…”

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.400
“Just a loon.”

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.409
This is perhaps the closest we get to a direct
acknowledgement of The Wendigo, but there

00:06:17.409 --> 00:06:19.640
are a couple more references later on.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.310
When we get to the burial ground, an overhead
shot shows that it uses the same concentric

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:33.629
circles layout as the Pet Sematary, but not
quite the same as the spiral layout described

00:06:33.629 --> 00:06:34.629
in the book.

00:06:34.629 --> 00:06:38.949
Louis buries Church, and to his dismay, Church
returns from the dead, only now with a much

00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:40.340
nastier attitude.

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.081
Louis doesn’t care though, as long as he
doesn’t have to explain Church’s death

00:06:43.081 --> 00:06:44.081
to Ellie.

00:06:44.081 --> 00:06:47.949
He goes to meet them at the airpor-- wait
a minute, did he pick these from his wife’s

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:48.949
garden?

00:06:48.949 --> 00:06:52.050
Oh and also these are yellow flowers, meaning
someone’s about to die.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.699
And the winner is… it’s Missy Dandridge.

00:06:54.699 --> 00:06:55.699
Why?

00:06:55.699 --> 00:06:57.940
Why couldn’t she have married a doctor?

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:01.389
Rachel stays home from the funeral with Gage,
still not able to overcome her fear of death

00:07:01.389 --> 00:07:04.139
because of what happened to her sister Zelda
when she was young.

00:07:04.139 --> 00:07:08.259
She totally misses out though, because Stephen
King makes a cameo appearance as the minister!

00:07:08.259 --> 00:07:11.909
Rachel finally opens up to Louis about how
her sister suffered from spinal meningitis,

00:07:11.909 --> 00:07:16.120
and the whole experience was extremely scarring
for her, right down the creepy painting in

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:17.120
her parents’ house.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.370
If you thought your sister dying was hard
Rachel, ohhh just wait, because this truck

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:26.240
driver can’t control his speed when he’s
blasting Sheena Is A Punk Rocker by The Ramones.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.169
The song choice is a little nod to the use
of Blitzkrieg Bop, the Ramones song that seems

00:07:30.169 --> 00:07:32.440
to be stuck in Louis’s head all throughout
the novel.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:37.349
He’s constantly thinking about the lyrics,
“Hey, ho, let’s go”, I think it’s

00:07:37.349 --> 00:07:41.129
a sign of the town manipulating him, because
the lyrics always seem to pop into his head

00:07:41.129 --> 00:07:44.379
when he’s thinking of digging up Gage to
move him to the Mic Mac grounds.

00:07:44.379 --> 00:07:48.090
This is because the Wendigo has the power
to put those thoughts into Louis’s head,

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:51.870
because as long as he’s in Ludlow, as long
as he’s close enough to the cursed ground,

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.810
he remains under its control.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:57.020
Remember how I said that Gage’s fascination
with trucks wasn’t the only thing that caused

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:58.310
him to wander into the street?

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:01.800
Well, during what I have come to call “the
scene”, as soon as Gage lets go of the kite

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.690
a huge gust of wind blows it into the street,
and you can actually see items flying off

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:06.860
the picnic table.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:08.030
This is no coincidence.

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:11.669
It’s similar to how in the book the truck
driver says he wasn’t speeding, but gets

00:08:11.669 --> 00:08:15.379
the sudden urge to drive recklessly as soon
as he crosses over into Ludlow.

00:08:15.379 --> 00:08:19.560
Keeping up with the yellow motif, Gage Creed’s
final outfit is a pair of yellow overalls.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:24.169
Despite what I said I earlier, I actually
do feel bad for him now that I see that he’s

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:25.169
a Cubs fan.

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:27.920
Everyone type [F] in the comments to pay your
respects to Gage.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:35.360
Gage’s casket is decorated with yellow flowers,
and things go from worse to even more worse

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:38.500
when Rachel’s father starts a fight with
Louis at the viewing ceremony.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:42.560
After the funeral, Jud realizes that Louis
may be getting the idea to put Gage up in

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:46.080
the Mic Mac burial ground, so he tells him
a story that he withheld the first time they

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:50.500
went up there, the story of Timmy Baterman,
a man who came back as a monster after being

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:51.500
buried there.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:55.630
In the flashback, Timmy Baterman shouts the
phrase “love dead dislike living”, a reference

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:58.520
to the horror classic, Bride of Frankenstein.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:05.340
“I love dead dislike living.”

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:09.330
Louis sends his family ahead to Chicago once
again, claiming he needs to close some things

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:12.930
up at work, but really plotting to dig up
Gage and attempt to revive him at the Mic

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:13.930
Mac grounds.

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:17.640
He goes to the cemetery where Gage rests in
broad daylight for some reason and Pascow

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:21.300
appears to him, standing in front of a tomb
with the name Thomas C Farr.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:25.320
I almost wonder if the “see far” is a
reference to Ellie’s psychic abilities,

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.630
like how she has the dream about Church dying,
and the dream about Louis dying even though

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:31.190
she is across the country when these events
take place.

00:09:31.190 --> 00:09:35.200
Also, these flowers around Gage’s grave
completely change from shot to shot, leaving

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.470
only… the yellow flowers.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:41.310
Ellie sees Pascow in her dream, and when Rachel
puts 2 and 2 together, she senses something

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:45.320
is wrong and rushes for the first flight home
to get to Louis, but not before having another

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:47.360
vision of her sister Zelda.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:53.120
“Gage and I will get you…

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:56.960
For letting us die.”

00:09:56.960 --> 00:10:01.510
Meanwhile, Louis takes Gage’s body to the
area beyond the Pet Sematary.

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:06.240
Listen to this laughter heard echoing through
the woods.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:15.020
Maybe that’s actually one of the loons that
Jud had mentioned, or it could also be a ghost

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:16.720
of one of the Mic Mac tribesman.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:19.940
This is the point at which Louis comes face
to face with the Wendigo in the book.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:23.570
As I mentioned, the creature doesn’t appear
in the film, but not showing it is actually

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:27.690
very unsettling, because if you are familiar
with the story, you kind of know that’s

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:31.520
it’s just off screen during this scene,
and I find that kind of haunting.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:35.620
Rachel rents a car to transport her the final
stretch back home, but suddenly blows a tire

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:36.910
when she gets close.

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:40.460
Like the wind that blew the kite into the
road, I feel like this tire popping is no

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:41.460
coincidence.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:45.330
She ironically hitches a ride with an Orinco
truck driver, however, she doesn’t realize

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:50.510
that she’s on the road to her death, which
is reflected in the number 666 on the right

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:51.510
side of the truck.

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:54.070
The last Wendigo reference shows up in Jud
Crandall’s room.

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:58.170
The Wendigo is described as being an extremely
tall bipedal creature with a stag’s head

00:10:58.170 --> 00:10:59.170
and antlers.

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:02.640
The candleholder on Jud’s mantle takes this
form, and could represent the fact that the

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.430
evil spirit is in this room right now, looking
at Jud, which it is, in the form of Gage,

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:11.370
who has come back as a zombie, and also in
the form of Church, who distracts Jud before

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:13.760
Gage finishes him off with his father’s
scalpel.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:16.000
Have you hugged your MD today?

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:19.130
Rachel finally makes it home, but the sound
of Zelda’s voices sends her into Jud’s

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:20.330
house instead of her own.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:21.330
“Rachel!”

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:24.590
She goes up into Jud’s room, where she hallucinates
her son’s image to be that of Zelda.

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:28.300
Then when she finally sees Gage for what he
is, she’s wearing the outfit from the creepy

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:32.220
painting in her parents house, the place where
she watched her sister die.

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:36.220
The outfit on Gage and the painting also matches
what Zelda wore on her deathbed.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:40.010
This is in line with the actions of the stories
of those who came back from the burial ground,

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:43.180
like Timmy Baterman and Hanratty the Bull
from the book.

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:46.950
The spirit that has taken over Gage’s body
knows the secrets of Rachel’s past and is

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:50.770
using this costume to bring up the repressed
tournament of her childhood.

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:54.490
She is unable to see past her son’s form
however, and she becomes his next victim.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:57.220
Louis wakes up the sound of Rachel’s screams.

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.480
He comes down and sees the footprints, filling
him in on what has happened.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:04.550
Rachel’s father calls to make sure she made
it, and just like the lied to Ellie after

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:08.480
Church’s death and told her that her cat
was fine, he lies and tells Mr. Goldman that

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:09.550
Rachel is still asleep.

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:14.160
Then he gets a call from Gage at Jud Crandall’s
house and realizes that he too has been possessed

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:15.410
by the evil spirit.

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:16.880
He knows what he must do.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:21.200
“Forgive me master.

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:22.040
But I must go all out.

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:23.360
Just this once.”

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:36.640
“Today is Thanksgiving Day for cats.”

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:47.280
"Play dead.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:49.520
BE DEAD!"

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:08.880
So with everyone dead, that leaves us with
only two more Things You Missed remaining.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.620
Louis burns down Jud’s house, just as Jud
burned down the Baterman house to cleanse

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:14.620
the evil all those years ago.

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:17.861
But Louis is deranged at this point, and he
believes that if he takes his late wife to

00:13:17.861 --> 00:13:20.790
the burial ground right away she can still
come back normal.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:24.640
To pass the time waiting for her to get back,
he plays solitaire on the kitchen floor.

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:28.930
When Rachel arrives, he has a Jack, and Queen
and a 2 flipped up, to represent those who

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:32.030
died, Jud, Rachel and the two year old Gage.

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:36.120
Unfortunately for the Doc, Rachel comes back
as a zombie, and the movie ends just before

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:37.730
she stabs him in the back.

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:42.050
Cue the end credits with an original song
by the Ramones as one final callback to the

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:44.740
constant Ramones references in Stephen King’s
novel.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:49.400
This song is actually a certifiable bop -- maybe
one of the last great Ramones songs actually.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:54.010
OK I was listening to that Ramones song, and
look what YouTube decided to autoplay.

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:55.010
That is savagery.

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:56.990
That is pure savagery YouTube.

00:13:56.990 --> 00:14:02.040
So that’s it for the Things You Missed in
the 1989 adaptation of Pet Sematary -- however,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:06.070
for some reason, there’s a sequel (even
though the book does not have a sequel, like

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:07.070
at all).

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:09.040
And for some other reason, there’s a remake
coming out.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:13.040
I’ll be covering both of those movies here
on Things You Missed in the next couple weeks,

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:18.110
so remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring that deathbell for

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:21.010
notifications and I’ll see you in the next
one.

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:23.210
Assuming we both survive.

